                # Create virtual environment\nL232:     source .venv/bin/activate    # Activate environment\nL233:     make setup                   # Auto-detect platform and install dependencies\nL234: ## Usage\nL235: \nL236: Platform Detection:\nL237: \nL238:     make platform               # Show detected platform\nL239:     make info                   # Show device information\nL240: \nL241: Server Evaluations:\nL242: \nL243:     make server-mmlu            # MMLU benchmark\nL244:     make planner                # Planner evaluation\nL245: \nL246: Tegra Evaluations:\nL247: \nL248:     make tegra-base             # Base MMLU evaluation\nL249:     make tegra-budget           # Budget evaluation\nL250:     make tegra-synthetic        # Synthetic benchmarks\nL251: Individual Benchmarks:\nL252: \nL253:     make prefill                # Prefill experiments (Tegra)\nL254:     make decode                 # Decode experiments (Tegra)\nL255: \nL256: Results: All outputs saved to `data/` directory with timestamp subdirectories.\nL257: \nL258: Help: `make help` for all available targets.\nL259: ## Analytical Models\nL260: \nL261:     python extract_io_length.py    # Extract I/O length data\nL262:     python plot_sweep.py           # Generate sweep plots\nL263:     python interactive.py          # Interactive analysis\nL264:     python latency_model.py        # Latency modeling\nL265: \nL266: ## Missing packages after setup? Try manual install with your .venv activated and in project root.\nL267: \nL268:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL269: \nL270: ## About\nL271: \nL272: Optimizing Reasoning LLM Deployment on Edge GPUs\nL273: ### Topics\nL274: \nL275: cite71†edge-computing cite72†edgereasoning cite73†llm cite74†llm-inference cite75†nvida-jetson L276: \nL277: ### Resources\nL278: \nL279: cite76†Readme L280: \nL281: cite77†BSD-3-Clause license L282: \nL283: cite78†Activity L284: \nL285: cite79†Custom properties L286: \nL287: ### Stars\nL288: \nL289: 2 stars\nL290: \nL291: ### Watchers\nL292: \nL293: 0 watching\nL294: \nL295: ### Forks\nL296: \nL297: cite80†1 fork L298: \nL299: cite81†Report repository L300: \nL301: ## Releases\nL302: \nL303: ## Packages\nL304: \nL305: ## Contributors\nL306: \nL307: ## Languages\nL308: \nL309: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.01310] From Pixels to Cooperation Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning based on Multimodal World Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01310)\nciteturn4859view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn4858view0\",\"lineno\":42}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Multiagent Systems\nL7: \nL8: This paper has been withdrawn by Sureyya Akin\nL9: \nL10: [Submitted on 3 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 11 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL11: # Title:From Pixels to Cooperation Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning based on Multimodal World Models\nL12: \nL13: Authors:cite6†Sureyya Akin , cite7†Kavita Srivastava , cite8†Prateek B. Kapoor , cite9†Pradeep G. Sethi , cite10†Sunita Q. Patel , cite11†Rahu Srivastava L14: \nL15: View a PDF of the paper titled From Pixels to Cooperation Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning based on Multimodal World Models, by Sureyya Akin and 4 other authors\nL16: \nL17: cite12†No PDF available, click to view other formats L18: > Abstract:Learning cooperative multi-agent policies directly from high-dimensional, multimodal sensory inputs like pixels and audio (from pixels) is notoriously sample-inefficient. Model-free Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) algorithms struggle with the joint challenge of representation learning, partial observability, and credit assignment. To address this, we propose a novel framework based on a shared, generative Multimodal World Model (MWM).\nL19: Our MWM is trained to learn a compressed latent representation of the environment's dynamics by fusing distributed, multimodal observations from all agents using a scalable attention-based mechanism. Subsequently, we leverage this learned MWM as a fast, \"imagined\" simulator to train cooperative MARL policies (e.g., MAPPO) entirely within its latent space, decoupling representation learning from policy learning.\nL20: We introduce a new set of challenging multimodal, multi-agent benchmarks built on a 3D physics simulator. Our experiments demonstrate that our MWM-MARL framework achieves orders-of-magnitude greater sample efficiency compared to state-of-the-art model-free MARL baselines.\nL21: We further show that our proposed multimodal fusion is essential for task success in environments with sensory asymmetry and that our architecture provides superior robustness to sensor-dropout, a critical feature for real-world deployment.\nL22: Comments:  | We have identified critical issues in the code implementation that severely deviate from Algorithm 1, invalidating all experimental results and conclusions. Despite exhaustive efforts to correct these issues, we find they fundamentally undermine the paper's core claims. To uphold academic integrity and prevent misinformation, we are withdrawing this manuscript\nL23: Subjects:  | Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\nL24: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.01310 [cs.MA]\nL25:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.01310v2 [cs.MA] for this version)\nL26:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01310†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Sureyya Akin [cite16†view email ]\nL30: cite5†[v1] Mon, 3 Nov 2025 07:44:56 UTC (1,419 KB)\nL31: [v2] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 01:34:30 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text l